Marc Goodman wrote on Sat, 21 Feb 2004 06:27:50 GMT:

> Case 1: Knowing the consequences of an action requires special knowledge
> only obtainable through experience or spoiling.  In this case, prompting
> the player is itself a "spoil," since the character could legitimately
> not know the consequences of the action.

I'm not sure I agree with your classification system. I'd count such a
prompt as gathered experience rather than "spoil", as it happens
in-game. I do get the point however, and agree that, generally, such
"spoils" should not happen. I think I can demonstrate that they do not
happen in your examples:

> Case 2: Knowing the consequences of an action requires special knowledge
> only obtainable through experience or spoiling, but the game gives it
> away anyway by prompting.  E.g., Stanislav's example of applying a
> wand and the resulting prompt about breaking the wand

By telling the game "apply wand" you actually underspecify the only
available apply-action, "break wand". The player has no way to know
that this will be the "outcome," and this lack of knowledge is quite
distrinct from lack of experience/spoilage with wands (e.g. they may
well know/assume that braking a wand of death is not desirable, but
still try to apply such a wand.). That's because this "outcome" is not
the effect of something the player actually does, instead *decides*
what the player does. Essentially, an effect of the interface.

(BTW, wand-braking was, initially, your example.)

> or when you go up on level 1 and the game prompts you to be sure
> because there is no turning back.

This example fits your classification. Note however that this behavior
follows from the fact that there's no out-of-dungeon terrain built in
the game (except for the Planes).  Theoretically, you should be able
to go out, take a walk for what it's worth, and return. As the game
does not implement the outside world, it reacts in this way, assuming
that the only action you might want is to escape (or ascend, if you
have the Amulet). It would be impolite to "force" that assumption on
the player, without warning them that "there will be no return." What
the game actually reveals is this lack of an outside world, which is
hardly surprising.

> Or when you are a wizard and the game asks you if you want to
> continue reading a difficult spellbook.

But this does not reveal anything that is not intended. NetHack has
the habit of braking actions when new information becomes available
and wizards do have the ability to judge the difficulty of spells by
looking at them.

> Case 3: Knowing the consequences of an action requires no special
> knowledge and the game prompts you anyway.  E.g., when you accidentally
> run into a peaceful monster without stormbringer and the game
> prompts you as to whether you really want to strike the monster.
> Or when you #pray and the game asks if you really want to pray.

These confirmations can be turned on and off using some options, so
one could indeed conclude that they are solely part of the interface.
That's not the case however--only *disabling* them is an interface
feature.

This is best demonstrated by the properties of Stormbringer--wielding
it removes your option of attack confirmation.

(In contrast, how the confirmation is performed is purely an interface
issue: you may click a button in a dialogue box, type 'y', type
"yes[enter]", etc.)

> I don't really expect to convince anyone, as TDTToE.  We all
> know Nethack is perfect exactly as it is and there's no need to
> ever go past 3.4.3 :)

Actually, I believe it started when you denied the current nature of
conducts in <i8BZb.27840$Xp.111117@attbi_s54>. In the beginning you
didn't state this as a YANI, or rather YAOI (everyone's beloved
abbriviation) with new (?) arguments to change an aspect of gameplay.

I do have the impression that you will not convince anyone that the
game has an interface defect that could be patched away with a
confirmation patch. If OTOH, you do agree with me that such a patch
will make the player play their own, perhaps better to their taste,
but easier, version of the game, then there's no need to argue that
point.

